WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
BIG TECH WON:
For decades, the goal of big tech has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:09.200
been clear and absolute: growth over everything&nbsp;
else. Regardless of what obstacle came up, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.560
were willing to push through no matter the cost.&nbsp;
Whether it was concerns regarding privacy or data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:20.080
collection or predatory pricing or monopolistic&nbsp;
practices or anti trust lawsuits or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:26.080
big tech kept charging forward hoping to maximize&nbsp;
their user count. And for the longest time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.160
this strategy worked. The more they pushed, the&nbsp;
further they were able to reach. They were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:37.040
convince whole new demographics and even countries&nbsp;
to adopt their technology. And over time, all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.680
this growth has allowed big tech to be some of the&nbsp;
most powerful, influential, and wealthy companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:48.960
in the entire world, but it seems that big tech&nbsp;
is finally facing a challenge that they can’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.480
brute force their way through: market saturation.&nbsp;
This was historically something that most tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.000
companies did not even consider simply because&nbsp;
their target market was virtually everyone with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.600
an internet connection. But, guess what? Everyone&nbsp;
with an internet connection now already uses most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.960
of the popular services offered by big tech. Take&nbsp;
WhatsApp for example. In Brazil, they control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:19.120
98.9% of the market. In Indian, they control&nbsp;
97.9%. In Italy, they control 97%. In Argentina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.640
they control 96% and so on and so forth. And&nbsp;
it’s not like they can grow in the countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.480
where they don’t control nearly a 100% either&nbsp;
because the rest of the market in these countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:35.160
aren’t exactly open. They’re just controlled by&nbsp;
another big tech offering whether that be iMessage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:42.320
or Android Messaging and this is the case with&nbsp;
basically every major industry. Chrome has 3.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.720
billion users, Android has 3.3 billion users,&nbsp;
YouTube has 2.7 billion users, Facebook has 2.9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.400
billion users, Instagram has 2.3 billion users,&nbsp;
and on and on ad on. And when you combine all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.840
of these market shares, you’ll see that big tech&nbsp;
already controls virtually the entire internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:08.040
In fact, just Google, Netflix, Facebook, Apple,&nbsp;
Amazon, and Microsoft control 57% of all global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:22.177
internet traffic. So, safe to say that big&nbsp;
tech has officially won the game, so what now?

00:02:22.177 --> 00:02:24.120
STAGNATION IS A KILLER:
Big tech reaching market saturation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.480
may not seem like a big deal. After all, how hard&nbsp;
could it be to just be the king of the hill and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:35.160
make billions every week right? Well, that would&nbsp;
be true if making billions every week was truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.080
the end goal for these companies. For most of&nbsp;
these companies, there is no concrete end goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:46.280
Their goal was to just grow infinitely. And given&nbsp;
that tech is able to reach a larger demographic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:52.200
than any previous industry, this strategy was&nbsp;
viable for decades. But, now that they’ve reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:59.240
market saturation, the goal becomes a lot harder.&nbsp;
Now, you’re probably wondering, why does that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.320
matter? Couldn’t these companies just hang up&nbsp;
their hats and call it day and enjoy market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.880
saturation? Well, they could definitely hang up&nbsp;
their hats but they wouldn’t really be able enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.840
it because many of these companies would end up&nbsp;
losing hundreds of billions of dollars because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:19.720
only thing holding up their massive valuations is&nbsp;
infinite growth. Take Apple for example. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:26.240
currently worth just under $3 trillion but on an&nbsp;
annual basis, they only make a little over a $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.800
billion. Now, that’s obviously an insane amount of&nbsp;
money but what we’re really concerned about is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.280
ratio between their valuation and their annual&nbsp;
earnings or their PE ratio which is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.720
about 30. In other words, if you were to buy the&nbsp;
entirety of Apple right now and they stop growing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:49.680
it would take you 30 years to make all your&nbsp;
money back. And even then, on your $3 trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:56.880
investment, you’d only be making a 3.3% yield on&nbsp;
an annual basis. Aka, you took a $3 trillion 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.360
year long gamble with Apple just to end up making&nbsp;
less than a high interest savings account or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.640
government bond. As you can see, investing in&nbsp;
big tech at these valuations only makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.240
if you think that they’re gonna continue doubling&nbsp;
and tripling and quadrupling. If you think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:16.320
they’re just gonna stay the same, it’s a terrible&nbsp;
investment. For it to make sense to invest in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.840
stagnant Apple, their valuation would have to&nbsp;
come way down. Usually, the standard PE ratio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.360
for mature companies with stagnant bottomlines&nbsp;
is 10. A lot of mature companies actually have PE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:36.520
ratios less than 10. Ford is at 6.78, Verizon is&nbsp;
at 7.55, and United Airlines is at just 4.56, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.880
let’s give Apple the benefit of the doubt and call&nbsp;
it 10. This would mean that an investment in Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:47.520
would pay back in 10 years. Aka, the annual yield&nbsp;
would be about 10% or a good amount higher than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.960
savings accounts and bonds making the investment&nbsp;
worth the risk. But, for Apple to have a PE ratio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.400
of 10, their valuation would have to fall to&nbsp;
$1 trillion which is obviously still a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:05.040
amount but it’s also $2 trillion less than their&nbsp;
current valuation. And it’s the same case with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.520
the rest of big tech. For Microsoft to come to PE&nbsp;
ratio of 10, they would have to shed $2 trillion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.800
Google would have to shed $1 trillion. Amazon&nbsp;
would have to shed $1.3 trillion. And Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.480
would have to shed $600 billion. And I think now&nbsp;
would be a good time to mention that Apple’s net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:28.440
income has already been stagnating for 2 years,&nbsp;
Google’s has been declining for 2 years, Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:34.000
was briefly losing money, and Meta’s net income&nbsp;
was straight up cut in half. The only big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.360
company that has managed to continue growing is&nbsp;
Microsoft and even that’s marginal. But, we don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:52.389
even have to speculate as to what would happen&nbsp;
because this has already happened in the past.

00:05:52.389 --> 00:05:54.880
GEN 1 TECH:
This transition from hyper growth to stagnation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.960
happens in basically every industry whether it&nbsp;
be railroad companies, banking, oil, automotives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:05.600
you name it. Eventually, the exponentiality&nbsp;
of the industry wears off and valuations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.960
expectations settle down. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;
tech as a whole is done for. There’s plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:17.160
innovations that have yet to materialize whether&nbsp;
that be VR or AI or whatever else. But, it’s more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.920
than likely that these new revolutions will be&nbsp;
led by a whole new generation of companies while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:28.800
current big tech gets left behind just like gen 1&nbsp;
tech companies. Take Intel for example. These guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.320
are one of the most influential tech companies&nbsp;
of history. They were basically the father of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:40.800
silicon wafers. Intel’s founder, Gordon Moore, was&nbsp;
literally the creator of Moore’s law. And as such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:48.520
they enjoyed tremendous growth between 1984&nbsp;
and 2000 of 90X. But, moving into the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.760
personal computer adoption peaked out. Everyone&nbsp;
who could possible get a computer already had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.760
one and PC sales started to crater. In fact, PC&nbsp;
sales have been crashing for 10 years now. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.840
isn’t to say that the semi conductor instury as&nbsp;
a whole stopped growing though. Qualcomm, Nvidia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.720
and TSMC have all figured out new market segments&nbsp;
to continue exponential growth. The peak of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:17.280
PC market, however, marked the top for Intel who&nbsp;
has been struggling to recover for the past 23&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.600
years. And they’re just one example. Dell was&nbsp;
crashing so hard that Michael Dell had to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.840
out of retirement, take the company private,&nbsp;
and completely pivot to save the company. HP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.480
on the other hand, ended up splitting into 2&nbsp;
separate companies and that’s just within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:38.880
PC manufacturing industry. Cisco was once the&nbsp;
world’s largest company, but after their peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:45.520
they would crash 80% and they have yet to recover&nbsp;
and it’s been 23 years. It’s the same case with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:52.760
AT&amp;T, and Verizon, and IBM, and Panasonic, and&nbsp;
Sony, and several other Gen 1 giants. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:57.160
interesting thing about all of these companies&nbsp;
is that none of them particularly dropped the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:02.280
ball. It’s not like Xerox or Yahoo where they&nbsp;
started losing market share and revenue started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.440
plummeting. No, these companies still&nbsp;
dominate their respective industries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:11.280
and fundamentally speaking, they’re actually&nbsp;
doing better than ever. Cisco, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.320
is now making double the revenue that they&nbsp;
were back in 2000. And Intel is almost making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.720
triple the profits. But, with their PE&nbsp;
ratios constantly falling towards 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:27.560
all of this fundamental growth has meant nothing&nbsp;
in terms of stock returns. The only gen 1 company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:33.080
that I can think of that actually overcame this&nbsp;
quicksand was Microsoft and it was by no means an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:39.400
easy battle. It took Microsoft exactly 17 years&nbsp;
to climb out of this bottomless pit and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:45.000
not even accounting for inflation. If we account&nbsp;
for inflation, it actually took Microsoft 18 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.160
to finally live up to their dotcom valuations.&nbsp;
And during this time period, Microsoft’s revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.480
had to grow 4 fold and their net income had to&nbsp;
grow 2.5 fold. In other words, if investors stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.360
believing in modern big tech’s growth story,&nbsp;
all of the companies that we know would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:06.800
to grow 2-3X in terms of revenue and net income&nbsp;
before the enjoy the same valuations. For Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.040
that means they’ll have to grow their net income&nbsp;
from $100 billion to $250 to $300 billion before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:17.840
they enjoy the same $3 trillion valuation. Big&nbsp;
tech CEOs are well aware of this and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:29.120
why they’re scrambling to keep investor morale&nbsp;
high and more importantly racing to monetize.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.200
THE RACE TO MONETIZE:
Likely the first big tech CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.360
to come to this realization was Satya Nadella.&nbsp;
When he tookover Microsoft, he was taking over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:43.680
from Steve Ballmer, one of the most controversial&nbsp;
tech CEOs of all time. Steve had grown Microsoft’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.000
revenue and profits by 3 fold, but Microsoft&nbsp;
stock had fallen 36% throughout his 14 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.000
of leadership. Satya saw this and decided that&nbsp;
he didn’t want to play this game of constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:00.600
creating the next big thing and achieving infinite&nbsp;
growth. That was clearly a young company’s game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:06.480
and Microsoft was very much a mature company. So,&nbsp;
what he did was transition Microsoft into becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.840
a tech holding company. Basically, he settled&nbsp;
for modest growth with core Microsoft products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:16.720
and started investing hundreds of billions in up&nbsp;
and coming companies with a bunch of exponential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:23.440
growth left. Just recently, they closed their $69&nbsp;
billion deal to acquire Activision Blizzard. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:29.880
the truth is, you can only do this so many times.&nbsp;
Eventually, you get so big that even a $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:36.800
billion acquisition doesn’t move the needle. The&nbsp;
second big tech CEO to realize this was Tim Cook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:42.960
In late 2018, he realized that iPhone sales were&nbsp;
not only starting to stagnate but decline. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.560
he decided to take control of the narrative&nbsp;
and stop reporting unit sales for all Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:53.640
products. Instead, he started focusing on services&nbsp;
like Apple Music and iCloud which was extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:59.240
successful. But, now they’re at nearly a billion&nbsp;
paying users and running out of iPhone users to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:05.360
monetize. The next big tech CEO to realize this&nbsp;
was Mark Zuckerberg. After the pandemic boom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.200
Mark realized that he had reached market&nbsp;
saturation with his social media empire. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:15.480
he decided to start digging for new gold.&nbsp;
He committed $100 billion to the Metaverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:20.080
changed the entire company’s name to Meta,&nbsp;
committed $33 billion to a new language model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:26.400
and tried to take on Twitter. But so far, none&nbsp;
of these new efforts have quite panned out. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:32.600
that brings us into Sundar Pichai, arguably the&nbsp;
last big tech CEO to realize this and as such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:38.080
his solution seems to be the most lack luster.&nbsp;
Google is basically just doubling down on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:44.720
cloud and packing in as many ads as they can.&nbsp;
But again, they can only pack in so many ads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:51.200
and take this so far which brings us into the&nbsp;
core issue at hand. Big tech has enjoyed massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:57.240
growth and tremendous returns in their pursuit&nbsp;
to reach everyone on the entire planet. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:02.040
they’ve actually done it and they’re facing the&nbsp;
real challenge which is not even growing but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:07.600
simply maintaining their sky high valuations&nbsp;
despite a stagnant user base. Every big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:12.200
company has chosen a different path to overcome&nbsp;
this new quick sand of high interest rates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:18.920
market saturation. Will their efforts actually pan&nbsp;
out? Only time will tell but looking at history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:24.800
it ain’t gonna be easy. Someone else&nbsp;
who’s facing an existential crisis is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:29.480
money burning startups who are struggling to get&nbsp;
more funding. Check out this video to learn more.

